A||N||9||varnster||Adjustable cam bolt (struts) how do I use it?||||9||
Z||000000||varnster||10-16-2001||10:54 PM||||I just replaced the struts, sensatrac. alighnment way off, the driver side has one<BR>of these weird looking bolts with two crecent looking washers, How do I use it to <BR>get my car half ass alighned before I take it<BR>to the pros? I read in the historicals and everyone said to use this, and does the pass<BR>side need one? It just has regular bolts.||||reg||9
Z||000001||varnster||10-17-2001||08:16 AM||||anyone!, bueller, bueller.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||10-17-2001||09:56 AM||||For camber, just use a regular level against the tire and turn the bolt with the offset washer until it is level. There should be a punched area that the washer can push against. This isn't exact but will get the camber close. For toe you can measure the outside egdes of the tires in the front and in the back. That is front outside edge of the rear left tire to right rear tire outside edge. Then measure the rear of the rear tire the same way. Once the measurements are taken adjust the toe until they are close to the same. That should get you close enough to safely drive to a shop for an accurate alignment.<P>It is better to have the alignment bolts on both side of the car.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 10-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||2birds||10-17-2001||11:21 AM||||I have Monroe Gasmatics, and one of the bolt holes (lower, I believe) is slotted.  No cam bolt should be needed if yout struts are like this.  Mine is lowered 1.5", and it's still easily alignable.<BR>Some alignment shops will tell you you need them anyway, since the GM struts don't have a slotted hole.  <BR>The alignment machine tells the tech he needs the bolts to fix it, and some of the techs don't know any better.||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||10-17-2001||12:28 PM||||The cam bolts also tend to hold the camber position while you tighten the bolts. Otherwise they want to snap back out of alignment before you can tighten them.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Oreif||10-17-2001||01:54 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 2birds:<BR><B>I have Monroe Gasmatics, and one of the bolt holes (lower, I believe) is slotted.  No cam bolt should be needed if yout struts are like this.  Mine is lowered 1.5", and it's still easily alignable.<BR>Some alignment shops will tell you you need them anyway, since the GM struts don't have a slotted hole.  <BR>The alignment machine tells the tech he needs the bolts to fix it, and some of the techs don't know any better.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>The bolts are used on slotted hole struts. The cammed washer pushes on the tang on the strut. The only reason they use it is to dial in the adjustmant accurately. Normally with out it they just bang it with a hammer and when "close enough" that is it. Also since the cammed washer is tight against the tang, the alignment holds better. So it cannot shift the alignment too easily when the tire hits a hazard. (Pothole, rock, debris, ricer coffee can exhaust, etc)<P>For the inexpensive amout of money it costs to get, it's well worth it to use them. The MOOG's actually have lines on them. Each line is suppose to represent 1/2 degree. So you can play around with the adjustment if you were racing on the road courses. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||10-17-2001||07:10 PM||||I just did this job 2 weeks ago...<P>The cam bolts are also available as Monroe # AK-30. You can get them from partsamerica.com or affiliated stores for about $8 a set. (Sound expensive??? they are grade 10 and 12 bolts... Serious hardware.)<P>The cam bolts make alignment go much more quickly and you'll get a more accurate job. <P>Alignment guys don't have time to waste farting with regular bolts. You'll be lucky just to have it inside acceptable adjustment range...<P>You can get a rough setting with a level. Most DIY people can't easily get to the nut and bolt with the wheel on... Use a magnetic level on the brake rotor. put a couple lug nuts on to hold the rotor snug to the bearing.<P>With the suspension hanging you want the bubble a little toward the line closest the rotor. (The suspension will lean out more when unloaded than when on the ground with the wheel on.) It hard to say exactly since there's some variation between levels. Better if you've got one of those dial type ones.<P>Ideally you put a level on it before changing the strut and then you only have to get close to original setting afterward. That eliminates the guessing.<P>No matter what you do you'll still need an alignment. If the shop isn't more than 10-15 miles don't worry too much. just keep the speed down on the way to the shop.<P>Monroe and Gabriel also have nice bumper/boots for the struts. Gabriel's are a full belows type that seals up the top of the strut. The bumpers on the top of theboot are important. If they are bad replaces them. especially on lowered cars. you can destroy the strut and mounting plate if the bumper is gone. (www.gabriel.com and <A HREF="http://www.monroe.com)" TARGET=_blank>www.monroe.com)</A> <P><BR>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">   <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||2birds||10-17-2001||10:11 PM||||Sounds like good info on the cam bolts.  I just hated spending the $20-$30 the shops try to charge you when you take it in.<BR>I see what you mean about the degreeing, also.  I did a 4-wheel alignment on my 97 Thunderbird the other night, and it uses eccentric washers for the adjustments.  Using a Hunter Laser rack, I was able to get a "perfect" (IMHO) alignment in just under 2 hours.  That included the shop guy showing me how to use the program.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Hippocrat||10-17-2001||10:20 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by varnster:<BR><B>anyone!, bueller, bueller.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ferris Buellers day off?<P>I just had to ask ... sorry it is not on topic.||||reg||1
Z||000009||varnster||10-18-2001||08:58 PM||||Thanks for all the help, and yes it was from <BR>buellers day off, a classic tell of youth against education, yes I have watched it, and<BR>no I HAVE NOT! ||||reg||6
